A||||17||Cameron||V8 vs Northstar vs 3800 SC||||1||
Z||000000||Cameron||04-23-2001||02:36 AM||||I have been wondering, I am eventually going to be doing an engine conversion on my '87 GT and am wondering with out starting an argument what the best engine is for them.  I have heard many things about all of them.  The biggest problem I have heard about with the v-8s is they tend to have more power then the weight of the car can handle and they tend to break traction easily.  However, if you are able to keep the traction I hear it's faster.  The v-8 I am looking at is the GM Crate ZZ4.  Anybody have specs regarding any of the engines: 0-60s, 0-100, 1/4 mile, etc.  I'm talking with out loads of mods and no NOS.  SuperCharged or RAM air is the extent of the mods.  Guesses?  Actual stats?  Just hoping for ideas.<P>( I am aware this is a controversial subject, but please refrain from attacking each other )<P><P>------------------<BR>Cameron<BR> <A HREF="http://Fiero.NetMARC.com" TARGET=_blank>http://Fiero.NetMARC.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||qwikgta||04-23-2001||03:31 PM||||4.5 or 4.9 caddie engine. Bolts up to the trannie with no modes. Check it out by typeing 4.5 or 4.9 in the search.  Look for Bubbajoexxx site, the best I have seen.<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html<BR>http://vafiero.com||||reg||1
Z||000002||RobertVol||04-23-2001||04:13 PM||||I think it really depends on your driving style. I had a 3800SC 3.8 and Phil still does.<P>I LOVED it and now wish I had not sold it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>but there really are so many options. The 3.4 DOHC is another good option but you did not mention it. I like the 3800 motor because it is a very reliable design. But then again the SBC 350 aint to shabby either.<P>Archie will more than likely come in and clarify the weight issue that you mentioned. It really is not that big of an issue. But I will let others that have more experience explain it <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>After all that is said my vote is the 3800 SC<P>A great ENGINE! <P><P>------------------<BR>Robert<BR>88GT 56k T TOP  <BR>North Florida Fiero Club<P>CLICK BELOW AND SEE OUR SITE !!! <A HREF="http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros" TARGET=_blank>http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros</A><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=819451&a=5959473&p=36934984&Sequence=1"> ||||reg||14
Z||000003||KRMFiero||04-23-2001||05:04 PM||||if i were you i would get the 3800 Series II Supercharged    then the northstar   then the chevy<P>another thing would be the older caddys (pre 94,   4.1,4.5 series||||reg||1
Z||000004||Black88GT||04-23-2001||05:30 PM||||4.1 and 4.5 are more hassle then they are worth IMHO.  I would do a 3.4 over either of them (same power, much less hassle).  <P>Out of those choices I would do a 3800 SC or if your willing to spend the bucks a 5speed Northstar.  Personally my next engine is a 3.4 DOHC turbo like Chris West has.  I dont like adding too much weight and SBC adds the most of any swap (except maybe the auto tranny Northstar as I heard the tranny was 200 lbs. more then a Getrag)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cameron||04-23-2001||05:42 PM||||I have done a bit of research on most of the engines...and I havn't ruled out the DOHC but didn't mention it because I havn't done much research on it.  Yes, you would be correct that the weight isn't too much of a problem, expecially since it's a mid engine car it balances the weight much more effectively then a front engine car.  The only thing I would wonder about the v8's is can you get that much power to the ground.  i mean the ZZ4 for example has 355 hp and 405 foot pounds of torque right out of the box no superchargers no blowers no mods.  That is a whole lot of power for a tiny car, admitedly they are heavy for their size.  I would only worry that it would be too much power and it would be tough to get the jump off the line, but after that I would imagine it would be hard to keep up with.  The problem could be that I have never ridden in a v8 fiero.  Maybe that would teach me something.  But again I don't know.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Black88GT||04-23-2001||05:47 PM||||Since Fiero's are mid-engined with a rear weight bias, wheel spin is no problem.  I have a 5 speed 3.4 with performance mods and with brand new BFG Comp T/A tires I can just barely squeel off the line (though I only tried once or twice I can't afford broken mounts).  Granted the 3.4 doesn't have the torque of a V8 but it still has a respectable amount.  Plus people with V8s generally don't like to spin wheels due to tranny issues.||||reg||1
Z||000007||88formula||04-23-2001||05:54 PM||||Maybe what you should do is go to some of the car shows in your area were there will be some of these cars. I did and drove a Supercharged 3800 series 2 engine. I absolutely loved it. It had so much torque that @ 40 mph in overdrive it would still kind of push you into the seat if you mashed the throttle with the five speed getrag. <P>I have never driven a Northstar fiero but I can tell you that the Northstar fiero swap is more complicated and more expensive based on what I have read here and elsewhere over the years especially if you want a manual transmission. <BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||terryk||04-23-2001||06:58 PM||||You're in California. You plan on driving this ZZ4 Fiero on the street? I assume you will run it with the matching TPI equipment?||||reg||1
Z||000009||Jay||04-23-2001||07:14 PM||||Cameron, if you are in Calafornia I think you will have problems with the Northstar.  Don't get me wrong, I have one in my car and I love it!  The power and torque are incredible and it is smooth  as silk.  Really, my engine is solid mounted and you would never be able to tell, no vibration.  The problem I think you will have if you go with the Northstar is California emissions requirements.  Is it true that you need a working check engine light?  If that is the case you will have to use the complete Cadillac harness, inside and out.  You can't scan a Northstar in a fiero unless the ECM recieves info from the climate control, and Instrument cluster.  It will also set codes if you don't have traction control or ABS.  Trust me we are all working on this.  In any other state (or country) I would say do the Northstar.  California...well, maybe not.  <P>Even with the automatic tranny my Northstar is putting out 255hp at the wheels (just got it dynoed) with little if any mods (shortened belt, K&N intake). It actually spiked at 302.6hp just befor the speed limiter kicked in at 150mph  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> .  On average though over six dyno runs it was 255hp. The weight is not a problem.  The Northstar is a powerful and potent engine that is "made" for the Fiero. The limp mode has never kicked on at the track or in everyday driving, only after a long run on the highway. If I were you I would check out custom programable computers and use a manual tranny.  Just my 2 cents worth.<P>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||saleenfiero||04-23-2001||07:35 PM||||With a northstar, what tranny hook up problems are there, do auto match right up or are the harder to match up than say, the legendary (sort of) 3.8?<P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||13
Z||000011||Richard Parnell||04-23-2001||08:46 PM||||I don't know who you've been talking to but with the tires I have on my SC w/v8 I rarely can break the wheels loose on my car, the car just squats down and literally launches.  I can tell you from first hand experience that against the Camaros and similar cars that I've run short little sprints from a dead start with that I can apply full throttle on take off and they can't.  They break loose every time and I'm gone.  I love it.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Jay||04-23-2001||10:53 PM||||I agree Richard!  I can take Mustangs, vettes, camaros, anything you can think of off the line.  I've only lost traction once and that was at the track.  The strip was a little moist (It had rained the day before)and all I did was sit there burning rubber.  Other than that on dry asphalt, It is over!!!Very little if any tire spin.<P>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||ray b||04-23-2001||11:21 PM||||3.5 v-6 twin cam is all alumimum and should bolt up.or quad 4 + turbo for the litest and best handeling.<BR>i heard that caddy is selling n* for 2500. to get them in hotrods but can't find the ads. a buddy said he saw in a kit car mag.||||reg||1
Z||000014||northstar87||04-23-2001||11:24 PM||||Hi,<BR>   I love to race Mustangs! Hey Jay, my car does consistent 13.6 @103 mph and I am running again at IRP on Sat.  Why haven't you run yours at the strip with the improvements yet?  Keep me posted!  I love the 3800 supercharged also.  That was my first engine conversion about 6 or so years ago.  It was a 5 speed. It was really fast but never took it to the track.  I sold it to do the Northstar.  I loved it so much I built another.  Just a nice setup.  By the way, I know the Auto trans is about 200 more lbs than other trans but it is made to handle the abuse of me and Jay.  Trust me on that one.  It needs to be strong!  <P>Chris Moore<BR>ASE Master Auto Tech and Auto Instructor<BR>1986 GT Convertible Northstar<BR>1987 GT Northstar<BR>1988 GT T-TOP 3.4 5speed<BR>1988 GT T-TOP stock||||reg||1
Z||000015||Cameron||04-23-2001||11:27 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Richard Parnell:<BR><B>I don't know who you've been talking to but with the tires I have on my SC w/v8 I rarely can break the wheels loose on my car, the car just squats down and literally launches.  I can tell you from first hand experience that against the Camaros and similar cars that I've run short little sprints from a dead start with that I can apply full throttle on take off and they can't.  They break loose every time and I'm gone.  I love it.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Amazing, only reason I was worried about the breaking loose is that there is so much power...like the ZZ4 has 355 bhp and 405 ft pnds of torque.  That's a lot of power for a car.  Unfortunately there aren't many shows in california.  There is one comming up that seems cool...<A HREF="http://www.cfog.org" TARGET=_blank>The Bay Area Fiero Fest</A>  But aside from that I mean...there aren't many shows here.<P>Guess that's another reason California Sucks!<P>--------------<P>Cameron<BR><A HREF="http://Fiero.netMARC.com" TARGET=_blank>Fiero.NetMARC.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by Cameron (edited 04-24-2001).]||||reg||1
